                <h1 class="mt-4 font-display text-[30px] leading-[1.15] font-extrabold tracking-[-0.01em] text-pretty sm:text-[38px]">Complete Habits With Siri And iOS Shortcuts</h1>

                <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-muted mt-4">
    <p>By <a href="https://habitkit.app/about" rel="author" class="font-semibold text-silver hover:text-accent focus-ring">Sebastian Röhl</a>, creator of HabitKit</p>
    <p class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-3 gap-y-1">
        <span>7 min read</span>
        <span>Published <time datetime="2026-08-26">Aug 2026</time></span>
                    <span>Updated <time datetime="2026-09-21">Sep 2026</time></span>
                            <span>Instructions for HabitKit 1.18.0</span>
            </p>
</div>

                
                <div class="help-body help-lead mt-6"><p>HabitKit adds seven actions to the Shortcuts app on iOS. Complete a build habit, clear a log, add an amount, write a note, read progress or open a habit. You can run them yourself, ask Siri, or add them to an automation such as finishing a workout.</p>
<p>All seven need iOS 17 or later. <strong>Log Amount</strong>, <strong>Log Habit Note</strong> and <strong>Get Habit Progress</strong> also accept a <strong>Date</strong>; leave it empty to use today.</p>
<p><img src="https://habitkit.app/help-assets/charcoal-ios-shortcuts.webp" alt="Shortcuts app panels showing HabitKit shortcuts and workout automations" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></p>
<p><em>The screenshots show an older action list. The seven actions and current English labels are listed below; labels follow your language settings.</em></p>
</div>

                <details class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-panel px-4 py-3 mt-6 lg:hidden">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer text-[14px] font-semibold text-silver focus-ring">On this page</summary>
        <nav aria-label="Article contents" class="mt-3">
