                                    <section id="what-you-need" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">What You Need</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><ul>
<li><strong>iOS 17 or later.</strong> If HabitKit's actions do not appear, check your iOS version first.</li>
<li><strong>The Shortcuts app</strong>, which ships with iOS.</li>
<li><strong>At least one habit</strong>, and not an archived one. Archived habits are left out of everything Shortcuts can see.</li>
<li><strong>Open HabitKit after creating or restoring a habit</strong>, so the Shortcuts picker receives the updated habit list.</li>
</ul>
<p>HabitKit does not have an equivalent Android automation integration. To log from an Android home screen, <a href="/help/widgets/add-a-habitkit-widget-to-your-home-screen">add a habit tracker widget</a>.</p>
<p>HabitKit has no Apple Watch app. A workout automation uses Apple's workout trigger to run a HabitKit action on iPhone.</p>
</div>
</section>
                                    <section id="the-seven-habitkit-actions-in-shortcuts" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">The Seven HabitKit Actions In Shortcuts</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><ul>
<li><strong>Mark Habit As Completed</strong> brings a build habit up to today's goal without reducing progress already above it. It cannot complete a quit habit; use Log Amount to record a slip or Clear Habit Log to clear one.</li>
<li><strong>Clear Habit Log</strong> clears today's recorded amount, including slips on a quit habit. It keeps the day's note.</li>
<li><strong>Increment Completion Of Habit</strong> adds a whole-number amount to today, defaulting to 1. Its shortcut tile is called <strong>Increment Habit</strong>. The <strong>Amount Of Completions</strong> field keeps working in existing automations.</li>
<li><strong>Log Amount</strong> adds an amount or sets the day's total. Choose <strong>Add</strong> or <strong>Set</strong> under <strong>Mode</strong>. It supports decimal measurements and an optional Date. On a quit habit, the amount records slips or the quantity you want to stay below.</li>
<li><strong>Log Habit Note</strong> writes a note without changing the recorded amount. Choose <strong>Append</strong> or <strong>Replace</strong> under Mode, and optionally choose a Date.</li>
<li><strong>Get Habit Progress</strong> reads the amount, goal, remaining amount, note, completion status, unit and date. Use its output in another action or a Shortcuts condition. It reads today unless you choose a Date; it does not return a streak count.</li>
<li><strong>Open Habit</strong> opens the selected habit in HabitKit.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every action has a <strong>Habit</strong> field. Choose the habit when building an automation so it can run without asking you. If you leave the field empty, the action asks which habit to use when it runs.</p>
<p>The first three actions always act on today. Increment uses the habit's existing goal rules; use Log Amount when you want to enter a precise total, a decimal measurement or an amount beyond the goal. Clear Habit Log sets the amount to zero without deleting the habit or its history.</p>
<p>If a habit stops appearing in the picker, open HabitKit and check whether it was archived or deleted. <a href="/help/data-backup-and-sync/automatic-backups-and-recovery">Automatic backups, and how to restore one</a> covers restoring an archived habit.</p>
</div>
</section>
                                    <section id="log-an-amount-or-note-for-a-past-day" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">Log An Amount Or Note For A Past Day</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><ol>
<li>Add <strong>Log Amount</strong> and choose the Habit.</li>
<li>Enter the <strong>Amount</strong> in the habit's saved unit. For a Measure habit, you can enter a decimal such as 2.5. Do not use thousands separators.</li>
<li>Choose <strong>Add</strong> to increase the recorded amount, or <strong>Set</strong> to replace the day's total. For example, Add 2.5 adds another 2.5; Set 2.5 makes the total 2.5.</li>
<li>Choose the <strong>Date</strong> you want to correct. Leave it empty for today.</li>
</ol>
<p>Use <strong>Log Habit Note</strong> with the same Date to add a reflection without changing the amount. Append keeps the existing note and adds the new text; Replace overwrites the note for that day.</p>
<p>Future days cannot be logged. Amounts in locked quit-habit history cannot be changed through Shortcuts either, although notes remain editable there. For the calendar controls and historical limits, see <a href="/help/getting-started/log-a-completion-and-fix-a-past-day">log a completion and fix a past day</a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
                                    <section id="complete-a-habit-automatically-after-a-workout" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">Complete A Habit Automatically After A Workout</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><ol>
<li>Open <strong>Shortcuts</strong> and tap <strong>Automation</strong> at the bottom of the screen.</li>
<li>Tap <strong>+</strong> to create a new automation, then choose a trigger. <strong>Workout</strong> is the one for a running or gym habit.</li>
<li>Set the details. For a run: workout type Running, and <strong>Ends</strong> rather than <strong>Starts</strong>. Turn on <strong>Run Immediately</strong> if you do not want to confirm it every time.</li>
<li>Tap <strong>Next</strong>, then <strong>New Blank Automation</strong>.</li>
<li>Tap <strong>Add Action</strong>, search for HabitKit, and choose <strong>Mark Habit As Completed</strong>.</li>
<li>Tap the <strong>Habit</strong> field and pick your habit.</li>
<li>Tap <strong>Done</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p><img src="https://habitkit.app/help-assets/charcoal-ios-shortcut-new.webp" alt="The Shortcuts screen offering New Blank Automation under Get Started" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></p>
<p><img src="https://habitkit.app/help-assets/charcoal-ios-shortcut-action.webp" alt="The finished automation holding Mark Habit As Completed with the Habit field set to Sport" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></p>
<aside class="md-callout"><span class="md-callout-label">Note</span><div><p>the labels in those steps are Apple's, not ours, and Apple moves them between iOS versions. The screenshots are from an older iOS. If a button has been renamed or moved, the shape is still the same: pick a trigger, choose a blank automation, add a HabitKit action, set the habit.</p></div></aside>
<p>The same structure works for any trigger Shortcuts offers. A time of day, arriving somewhere, opening an app, plugging in a charger. The HabitKit half of it never changes.</p>
</div>
</section>
                                    <section id="ask-siri-to-complete-a-habit" data-article-section class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-11">
    
    <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold text-pretty sm:text-[22px]">Ask Siri To Complete A Habit</h2>

    <div class="help-body mt-[18px]"><p>The actions include spoken phrases. Try one of these, replacing Reading with your habit's name where needed:</p>
<ul>
<li>&quot;Check off a habit in HabitKit&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;Add a completion to my habits in HabitKit&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;Check off habit Reading in HabitKit&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;Clear the log for Reading in HabitKit&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;Increment a completion for Reading in HabitKit&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;Log an amount for Reading in HabitKit&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;Log a note for Reading in HabitKit&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;Get progress for Reading in HabitKit&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;Open Reading in HabitKit&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>Siri reports the updated amount after a change, or the current progress when you ask for it. Quit-habit responses describe recorded slips. If you leave the habit out, Siri asks which one you mean.</p>
<p>If an action runs without the expected change, open HabitKit and check that the habit is active, then select it again in the action's <strong>Habit</strong> field. Mark Habit As Completed leaves an already-completed build habit unchanged, including progress above its goal. For a quit habit, use Log Amount or Clear Habit Log instead. If the problem continues, <a href="/help/troubleshooting/habitkit-will-not-open-or-shows-an-error">write in</a> with the action name, habit type and expected result.</p>
</div>
</section>
                
                                    <section id="sec-faq" data-article-section class="mt-12 scroll-mt-4 sm:mt-[52px]">
                        <h2 class="font-display text-[20px] font-extrabold sm:text-[22px]">Related Questions</h2>

                        <div class="mt-[18px] flex flex-col gap-2.5">
                                                            <details class="group rounded-[14px] border border-white/7 bg-panel px-5"  open >
                                    
                                    <summary class="flex list-none cursor-pointer items-center justify-between gap-4 py-4 text-[15px] font-bold text-foam focus-ring">
                                        <span class="text-pretty">Why can&#039;t I find &quot;Complete Habit&quot; in Shortcuts?</span>
                                        
                                        <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-[18px] leading-none text-dim group-open:rotate-45 motion-safe:transition-transform">+</span>
                                    </summary>
                                    <p class="pb-[18px] text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-muted text-pretty">Search for Mark Habit As Completed for a build habit. To clear today&#039;s logged amount, use Clear Habit Log, the current name of the old clearing action. Existing saved shortcuts keep working.</p>
                                </details>
                                                            <details class="group rounded-[14px] border border-white/7 bg-panel px-5" >
                                    
                                    <summary class="flex list-none cursor-pointer items-center justify-between gap-4 py-4 text-[15px] font-bold text-foam focus-ring">
                                        <span class="text-pretty">Can a Shortcut log a past day?</span>
                                        
                                        <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-[18px] leading-none text-dim group-open:rotate-45 motion-safe:transition-transform">+</span>
                                    </summary>
                                    <p class="pb-[18px] text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-muted text-pretty">Yes. Choose a Date in Log Amount or Log Habit Note to edit a past day. Get Habit Progress can read a chosen day too. The complete, clear and increment actions still act on today. Future days cannot be logged, and locked historical amounts remain read-only.</p>
                                </details>
                                                            <details class="group rounded-[14px] border border-white/7 bg-panel px-5" >
                                    
                                    <summary class="flex list-none cursor-pointer items-center justify-between gap-4 py-4 text-[15px] font-bold text-foam focus-ring">
                                        <span class="text-pretty">Is there anything like this on Android?</span>
                                        
                                        <span aria-hidden="true" class="text-[18px] leading-none text-dim group-open:rotate-45 motion-safe:transition-transform">+</span>
                                    </summary>
                                    <p class="pb-[18px] text-[14.5px] leading-[1.7] text-muted text-pretty">HabitKit&#039;s Shortcuts and Siri actions are iOS only. On Android, use a home screen widget to log a habit outside the app.</p>
                                </details>
                                                    </div>

                    </section>
